WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Tuscany. Why don't you put salt in your bread?&nbsp;
I have a few theories. One is that Florence is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.980
kind of far from the coastline, so they&nbsp;
didn't have access to salt. That doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.120
really hold up. There's a lot of inland places&nbsp;
that have salt in their bread. Another theory is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:17.220
that all the food eaten in this area is so extra&nbsp;
salty that eating it with bread, with no salt is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.000
actually like it balances it all out. Although,&nbsp;
there's lots of other parts of Italy that also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.900
eat similarly salty food, but they still put&nbsp;
salt in their bread. The last theory is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.720
Salt War. Back in like 1540, the Pope raised&nbsp;
taxes on salt in one important Italian city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.080
and the people were like, “%$@# that, we'll&nbsp;
just stop salting our bread.” “Oh, and now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.980
we're independent, Pope!” And the Pope was like,&nbsp;
“that's cute.” And had his army swiftly crush the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.720
rebellion. So now saltless bread is a tradition&nbsp;
carried on in the name of defiance. But again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.760
this story revolves around a specific city. And&nbsp;
saltless bread is found all over Tuscany. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.320
there's not a ton of historical evidence&nbsp;
supporting this sudden change in bread baking. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.640
there's references to saltless bread in Tuscany,&nbsp;
like hundreds of years before the Salt War. So,

